  "prediction": "The report discusses the Federal Emergency Management Agency's (FEMA) Fire Management Assistance Grants (FMAGs), which provide financial assistance to states and local governments for suppressing fires that exceed their resources. FMAGs are authorized under the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act (Stafford Act) and are administered by FEMA.\n\nFMAGs can be requested when wildfires exceed local resources, and they cover eligible costs for suppressing the fire and related expenses. The report answers frequently asked questions about FMAGs, including how they are requested, the evaluation process, and the types of expenses covered.\n\nTo request an FMAG, a state or local government must submit a request to FEMA, providing detailed information about the fire, including its size, location, and the resources required to contain it. FEMA evaluates the request based on the size and complexity of the fire, as well as the availability of state and local resources.\n\nFMAGs can be used to cover a range of expenses, including personnel costs, equipment rentals, and transportation. The report also discusses the differences between FMAGs and other types of federal assistance, such as emergency suppression assistance from the Forest Service.\n\nThe report concludes by noting that FMAGs are an essential tool for managing large wildfires and ensuring that local resources are not overwhelmed. By providing financial assistance, FMAGs help to ensure that fires are contained as quickly and effectively as possible, reducing the risk of damage to property and lives.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report discusses the Federal Emergency Management Agency's (FEMA) Fire Management Assistance Grants (FMAGs), which provide financial assistance to states and local governments for suppressing fires that exceed their resources. FMAGs are authorized under the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act (Stafford Act) and are administered by FEMA.\n\nFMAGs can be requested when wildfires exceed local resources, and they cover eligible costs for suppressing the fire and related expenses. The report answers frequently asked questions about FMAGs, including how they are requested, the evaluation process, and the types of expenses covered.\n\nTo request an FMAG, a state or local government must submit a request to FEMA, providing detailed information about the fire, including its size, location, and the resources required to contain it. FEMA evaluates the request based on the size and complexity of the fire, as well as the availability of state and local resources.\n\nFMAGs can be used to cover a range of expenses, including personnel costs, equipment rentals, and transportation. The report also discusses the differences between FMAGs and other types of federal assistance, such as emergency suppression assistance from the Forest Service.\n\nThe report concludes by noting that FMAGs are an essential tool for managing large wildfires and ensuring that local resources are not overwhelmed. By providing financial assistance, FMAGs help to ensure that fires are contained as quickly and effectively as possible, reducing the risk of damage to property and lives.",
  "score": 29.166666181979174,
  "answers": [
    "Section 420 of the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act (P.L. 93-288, hereinafter the Stafford Act) authorizes the President to \"declare\" a Fire Management Assistance Grant (FMAG). In the interest of saving time, the authority to make the declaration has been delegated to the Federal Emergency Management Agency's (FEMA's) Regional Administrators. Once issued, the FMAG declaration authorizes various forms of federal fire suppression assistance such as the provision of equipment, personnel, and grants to state, local, and tribal governments for the control, management, and mitigation of any fire on certain public or private forest land or grassland that might become a major disaster. This federal assistance requires a cost-sharing component such that state, local, and tribal governments are responsible for 25% of the expenses. This report answers frequently asked questions about FMAGs. This report will be updated as events warrant."
  ],
